                <span class="block font-serif text-[19px] font-semibold text-navy mb-1.5">{n.n}</span>
                <span class="block text-[15px] text-muted">{n.d}</span>
                <span class="block text-[14px] text-crimson font-semibold mt-2.5">Read the {n.n} page</span>
              </a>
            ) : (
              <>
                <h3 class="text-[19px] mb-1.5">{n.n}</h3>
                <p class="text-[15px] text-muted m-0">{n.d}</p>
              </>
            )}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-muted mt-7">
        There are a small number of Columbus submarkets we do not take on new business in. That is a
        judgment about where we can run a property well, not a comment on the neighborhood. Give us the
        address and we will tell you plainly, before you spend time on a quote.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SOURCE OF INCOME -->
  <section class="band band-wash" aria-labelledby="soi-h">
    <div class="wrap max-w-[900px]">
      <span class="eyebrow">Screening inside Columbus</span>
      <h2 id="soi-h" class="text-[clamp(28px,3.3vw,40px)]">Can a Columbus landlord refuse a housing voucher?</h2>
      <p class="text-[17.5px] text-muted mt-4 max-w-[44em]">
        No. Columbus adopted source of income protection in March 2021, effective that July. It is
        unlawful inside the city to refuse an applicant, or to change the price, terms, conditions or
        fees, because part of the rent will be paid by a Housing Choice Voucher, child support, spousal
        support or public assistance. A violation is a first-degree misdemeanor.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17.5px] text-muted mt-5 max-w-[44em]">
        The practical trap is the income multiple. If your criteria require income of three times the
        rent, the voucher portion is subtracted before the test runs. On a {usd(1500)} rent where a
        voucher covers {usd(900)}, the multiple applies to the {usd(600)} the resident actually pays,
        not to the full {usd(1500)}. Applying it to the full rent screens out voucher holders by
        arithmetic, which is exactly what the ordinance prohibits. Everything else in a screening
        standard still applies, and it has to apply identically to every applicant.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[14px] text-faint mt-5">
        [SOURCE NEEDED: current status of any Ohio state preemption litigation affecting the Columbus
        source of income ordinance as of September 2026. The ordinance is in force; the preemption
        question has been raised in commentary and we have not confirmed a controlling decision.]
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SERVICES -->
